% Section 3: Background and Problem Setup
% Phase-Modulated Compound Sparsity

\section{Background and Problem Setup}
\label{sec:background}

We formalize compound sparsity as the simultaneous application of attention head pruning and KV cache eviction, define the Interaction Index to measure their non-additive interaction, and introduce phase segmentation to localize the analysis across reasoning stages.

\subsection{Compound Sparsity Formulation}
\label{sec:compound-sparsity}

Consider a transformer language model $\mathcal{M}$ with $L$ layers, each containing $H$ attention heads.
Let $s \in (0,1)$ denote a unified sparsity budget applied to both compression axes.

\paragraph{Attention head pruning.}
Given per-head importance scores $\mathbf{w}^{(l)} \in \mathbb{R}^H$ for layer $l$, we construct a binary mask $\mathbf{m}_h^{(l)} \in \{0,1\}^H$ that retains the top-$(1{-}s) \cdot H$ heads by importance.
For head $j$ in layer $l$, the masked attention output becomes:
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{\mathbf{o}}_j^{(l)} = m_{h,j}^{(l)} \cdot \mathbf{o}_j^{(l)}
    \label{eq:head-mask}
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{o}_j^{(l)}$ is the original head output.
Head importance is computed as the Frobenius norm of the attention weight matrix: $w_j^{(l)} = \|\mathbf{A}_j^{(l)}\|_F$, where $\mathbf{A}_j^{(l)} \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times T}$ is the attention probability matrix for head $j$ at layer $l$ over a calibration input of length $T$.

\paragraph{KV cache eviction.}
For a sequence of length $T$, we construct a position mask $\mathbf{m}_k \in \{0,1\}^T$ that retains the top-$(1{-}s) \cdot T$ positions by importance, with position $0$ (BOS) always retained.
Evicted positions are blocked by setting their attention mask entries to zero:
\begin{equation}
    \tilde{a}_{ij} = a_{ij} \cdot m_{k,j}, \quad \forall i,j
    \label{eq:kv-mask}
\end{equation}
We evaluate two eviction strategies:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{Importance-based:} Position importance is the cumulative attention mass received across all heads and query positions, with position normalization to correct for causal attention bias:
    \begin{equation}
        \mathrm{imp}(j) = \frac{\sum_{l,h,i} a^{(l,h)}_{ij}}{T - j}
        \label{eq:kv-importance}
    \end{equation}
    where the denominator $(T - j)$ compensates for the fact that later positions can attend to fewer future tokens under causal masking \cite{thinkv2026}.
    \item \textbf{Random:} Positions (excluding BOS) are evicted uniformly at random.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Compound sparsity.}
Applying both compressions simultaneously yields a model $\mathcal{M}(s)$ where fraction $s$ of heads are pruned per layer \emph{and} fraction $s$ of KV positions are evicted.
The two axes interact: head pruning alters the attention distribution over surviving heads, which in turn changes the importance ranking used for KV eviction.

\subsection{Interaction Index}
\label{sec:interaction-index}

To quantify whether compound sparsity degrades performance more (or less) than the sum of its components, we define the \emph{Interaction Index} (II) following the factorial interaction framework from experimental design.

Let $\mathrm{PPL}_d$, $\mathrm{PPL}_h$, $\mathrm{PPL}_k$, and $\mathrm{PPL}_b$ denote the perplexity of the dense model, head-pruning only, KV-eviction only, and both applied simultaneously, respectively.
Perplexity is computed as $\mathrm{PPL} = \exp\!\bigl(\frac{1}{|S|}\sum_{t \in S} \mathcal{L}_t\bigr)$ where $\mathcal{L}_t$ is the per-token cross-entropy loss and $S$ is the set of evaluated positions.

We define the relative degradation ratios $R_h = \mathrm{PPL}_h / \mathrm{PPL}_d$, $R_k = \mathrm{PPL}_k / \mathrm{PPL}_d$, $R_b = \mathrm{PPL}_b / \mathrm{PPL}_d$, and the Interaction Index:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{II} = R_b - R_h - R_k + 1
    \label{eq:ii}
\end{equation}
Under additive independence, $R_b \approx R_h + R_k - 1$, giving $\mathrm{II} \approx 0$.
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item $\mathrm{II} > 0$: \textbf{super-additive} — compound sparsity degrades more than the sum of individual effects.
    \item $\mathrm{II} < 0$: \textbf{sub-additive} — compound sparsity is less harmful than expected.
    \item $\mathrm{II} = 0$: \textbf{additive} — no interaction between the two axes.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Choice of additive null.}
Eq.~\ref{eq:ii} posits an additive null $R_b = R_h + R_k - 1$, but one could equally assume multiplicative independence $R_b = R_h \cdot R_k$, treating the two axes as independent survival probabilities.
We adopt the additive form because head pruning and KV eviction operate on disjoint model dimensions---structural (which heads to keep) versus positional (which tokens to retain).
When the two mechanisms act independently, each contributes an additive increment $\Delta R$ to the perplexity ratio: the heads removed by pruning and the positions removed by eviction affect non-overlapping information pathways, so their degradations sum rather than multiply.
At the sparsity levels studied ($s \leq 0.30$), this non-overlap assumption holds empirically (\S\ref{sec:experiments}).
We report the multiplicative residual $R_b / (R_h \cdot R_k)$ alongside II in Appendix~\ref{app:multiplicative-null}; the qualitative conclusions are unchanged under either null model.

We evaluate II on 50 problems from MATH-500 \cite{hendrycks2021math}, stratified across difficulty levels.
Per-problem II values are aggregated via the median (rather than the mean) due to heavy-tailed distributions---e.g., the mean/median ratio reaches $33.5\times$ for the low-sparsity phase at $s{=}0.30$ (\S\ref{sec:experiments})---where a single outlier can dominate the mean.
Bootstrap confidence intervals ($B=10{,}000$ resamples) are computed at the problem level.

\paragraph{Severity gradient.}
To capture how II varies across reasoning phases, we define the \emph{severity gradient}:
\begin{equation}
    G = \frac{\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\mathrm{low}}}{\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\mathrm{high}}}
    \label{eq:gradient}
\end{equation}
where $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\mathrm{low}}$ and $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\mathrm{high}}$ are the median II in the low-sparsity and high-sparsity phases respectively.
$G \gg 1$ indicates that compound degradation concentrates in low-sparsity phases; $G \approx 1$ indicates uniform degradation.
$G$ is defined only when $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\mathrm{high}} > \varepsilon$ for a small threshold $\varepsilon > 0$; when $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\mathrm{high}} \approx 0$ (ceiling effect, where head pruning alone causes negligible degradation in high-sparsity phases), we report $G = \infty$.

\subsection{Phase Segmentation}
\label{sec:phase-segmentation}

Reasoning-capable LLMs produce token sequences with heterogeneous attention patterns: some segments exhibit concentrated attention (few tokens dominate), while others distribute attention broadly.
We partition each generation into three phases based on the \emph{attention sparsity profile}, following the methodology of ThinKV \cite{thinkv2026}.

\paragraph{Attention sparsity.}
For each query position $i$ and head $h$, we compute the fraction of key positions receiving attention below $1\%$ of the row maximum:
\begin{equation}
    \sigma_i^{(h)} = \frac{1}{T}\sum_{j=1}^{T} \mathbf{1}\!\left[a_{ij}^{(h)} < 0.01 \cdot \max_k a_{ik}^{(h)}\right]
    \label{eq:sparsity}
\end{equation}
The per-token sparsity $\sigma_i = \frac{1}{H}\sum_h \sigma_i^{(h)}$ averages across heads.

\paragraph{Quantile-based segmentation.}
We partition tokens into three equal-sized groups by the quantiles of $\{\sigma_i\}_{i=1}^{T}$:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
    \item \textbf{High-sparsity phase} (top tercile): attention concentrates on a few tokens; heads are highly redundant, and pruning incurs minimal information loss.
    \item \textbf{Mid-sparsity phase} (middle tercile): intermediate attention patterns.
    \item \textbf{Low-sparsity phase} (bottom tercile): attention distributes broadly across many tokens; each head carries non-redundant information, making pruning costly.
\end{itemize}

This segmentation is purely data-driven and does not require access to model internals beyond attention weights.
It captures the heterogeneity in attention structure that arises naturally during extended reasoning, where the model alternates between focused retrieval (high sparsity) and exploratory cross-token integration (low sparsity) \cite{thinkingmi2025}.

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental-setup}

\subsection{Models}
\label{sec:models}

We conduct experiments on two 7B-parameter instruction-tuned models that share the Qwen2 backbone architecture (28 Transformer layers, 28 attention heads with 4-group GQA):

\paragraph{DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B} serves as our primary evaluation target. This model was distilled from DeepSeek-R1 \cite{deepseek-r1} and produces explicit chain-of-thought traces delimited by \texttt{<think>}$\cdots$\texttt{</think>} tags, yielding sequences with pronounced phase structure---extended reasoning followed by a concise solution. The structured generation pattern makes it well-suited for studying how sparsity interacts with distinct reasoning phases.

\paragraph{Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct} \cite{qwen2.5} provides a cross-architecture replication. Although it shares the same backbone family, its instruction tuning does not enforce explicit reasoning delimiters, producing a different distribution of attention sparsity across the generation trajectory. Replication on this model tests whether our findings generalize beyond distillation-induced phase structure.

Both models are loaded in \texttt{bfloat16} precision with eager attention (i.e., without FlashAttention) to permit direct manipulation of attention weight matrices during sparsity intervention.\footnote{An earlier \texttt{float16} configuration produced sporadic NaN values in Layer~27 of DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, affecting ${\sim}$8\% of logit positions. Switching to \texttt{bfloat16} eliminated all NaN occurrences; we additionally apply NaN-guarded filtering as a safety net.}


\subsection{Dataset}
\label{sec:dataset}

We evaluate on a 50-problem stratified subset of the MATH-500 benchmark \cite{math500}, sampling uniformly across problem categories (algebra, geometry, number theory, combinatorics, etc.) with a fixed seed for reproducibility. Mathematical reasoning tasks are a natural testbed for our study: they elicit long chain-of-thought sequences (up to 2048 tokens) in which the model alternates between exploratory reasoning and structured computation---phases that differ markedly in attention sparsity. Each problem is decoded greedily (\texttt{max\_new\_tokens}$=$2048) to eliminate sampling variance.


\subsection{Compound Sparsity Configuration}
\label{sec:sparsity-config}

We apply \emph{symmetric} compound sparsity: for a given sparsity level $s$, we prune the same fraction $s$ of attention heads and evict the same fraction $s$ of KV cache entries. We evaluate four sparsity levels $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$, spanning from mild to aggressive compression.

\paragraph{Sparsity conditions.} For each problem and each sparsity level, we compute perplexity under four conditions: (i)~\emph{dense}---no intervention; (ii)~\emph{attn-only}---head pruning without KV eviction; (iii)~\emph{kv-only}---KV eviction without head pruning; and (iv)~\emph{both}---simultaneous head pruning and KV eviction.

\paragraph{Eviction strategies.} We compare two KV cache eviction strategies:

\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Importance-based.} Each KV position receives a score equal to its cumulative attention weight summed over all query positions and heads. To correct for the inherent causal bias---earlier positions accumulate more attention simply because they are attended to by more queries---we apply position normalization:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:importance}
    \alpha_j = \frac{\sum_{i,h} a_{i,h,j}}{\texttt{seq\_len} - j}, \quad j \in \{1, \dots, T\},
\end{equation}
where $a_{i,h,j}$ is the attention weight from query~$i$, head~$h$ to key~$j$, and the denominator counts the number of queries that can attend to position~$j$ under causal masking. Position~0 (BOS) is always retained. The $T \cdot s$ positions with the lowest normalized scores are evicted.

During masked forward passes, we guard against numerical instability from the interaction of head masking and KV eviction: zeroed-out heads that would otherwise produce $\infty \times 0 = \text{NaN}$ are handled via selective replacement (\texttt{torch.where}) prior to the softmax, and any layer producing NaN outputs is skipped from the sparsity loss computation.

\item \textbf{Random.} KV positions (excluding BOS) are evicted uniformly at random, providing a strategy-agnostic baseline. Head pruning under the random condition selects heads via \texttt{torch.randperm} rather than by importance.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Head pruning.} Under importance-based eviction, heads are ranked by their Frobenius norm across the full attention matrix $\mathbf{A}_\ell \in \mathbb{R}^{H \times T \times T}$ per layer $\ell$, and the bottom-$s$ fraction is masked. Under random eviction, heads are selected uniformly at random.


\subsection{Phase Segmentation}
\label{sec:phase-seg}

We segment each generated sequence into three \emph{reasoning phases} based on the local attention sparsity of each token position. For each query position $i$, we compute per-head sparsity as the fraction of attention weights falling below $0.01 \times \max_j a_{i,h,j}$, then average across heads to obtain a scalar sparsity value per position. The resulting sparsity trajectory is partitioned into terciles via quantile boundaries: \emph{low-sparsity} (bottom third, corresponding to focused attention during structured computation), \emph{mid-sparsity}, and \emph{high-sparsity} (top third, corresponding to diffuse attention during exploratory reasoning). Contiguous segments shorter than $\texttt{min\_phase\_length}=10$ tokens are merged into adjacent phases to suppress noise.


\subsection{Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

\paragraph{Perplexity.} For each sparsity condition $c \in \{d, a, k, b\}$ and each phase, we compute segment-level perplexity as
$\text{PPL}_c = \exp\!\bigl(\tfrac{1}{|I|}\sum_{i \in I} \mathcal{L}_c(x_i)\bigr)$,
where $I$ is the set of token indices belonging to the phase and $\mathcal{L}_c(x_i)$ is the per-token cross-entropy loss under condition $c$.

\paragraph{Interaction Index.} The interaction index quantifies the synergistic (super-additive) effect of compound sparsity beyond the sum of individual interventions:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:ii}
    \mathrm{II} = \frac{\text{PPL}_b}{\text{PPL}_d} - \frac{\text{PPL}_a}{\text{PPL}_d} - \frac{\text{PPL}_k}{\text{PPL}_d} + 1.
\end{equation}
When $\mathrm{II} > 0$, the two sparsity mechanisms amplify each other; $\mathrm{II} = 0$ indicates additive (independent) degradation.

\paragraph{Severity Gradient.} To measure how the interaction varies across reasoning phases, we define the severity gradient:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:gradient}
    G = \frac{\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{low}}}{\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{high}}},
\end{equation}
where $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{low}}$ and $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{high}}$ are the median interaction indices over the low-sparsity and high-sparsity phases, respectively. We report medians rather than means because the II distribution is heavily right-skewed (mean/median ratios up to 43:1 at $s{=}0.30$), making the median a more robust central tendency estimator.


\subsection{Statistical Analysis}
\label{sec:stats}

All confidence intervals and significance tests are computed via the nonparametric bootstrap with $B = 10{,}000$ resamples \cite{efron1993bootstrap}.

\paragraph{Per-problem bootstrap.} For each sparsity level and strategy, we resample the 50 per-problem II values with replacement and compute the mean of each resample, yielding a 95\% percentile confidence interval for the population mean II.

\paragraph{Paired bootstrap.} To test whether the severity gradient differs between importance-based and random eviction, we pair observations by problem (same problem, same sparsity level) and bootstrap the difference in gradients. This controls for problem-level variance in difficulty and sequence length.

\paragraph{Unpaired bootstrap.} We additionally compute per-strategy gradient confidence intervals without pairing, to verify that the importance-based gradient is significantly greater than 1 (i.e., phase modulation exists) independently of the random baseline.

All tests yield $p < 0.0001$ at every sparsity level, confirming that the observed phase--strategy interactions are not artifacts of sampling variability.\footnote{Experiments were conducted on a server with 4$\times$ NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada 96\,GB GPUs, with one model--sparsity configuration per GPU.}

\subsection{Statistical Analysis}
\label{sec:stats}

All confidence intervals and significance tests are computed via the nonparametric bootstrap with $B = 1{,}000$ resamples \cite{efron1993bootstrap}.

\paragraph{Per-problem bootstrap.} For each sparsity level and strategy, we resample the 50 per-problem II values with replacement and compute the mean of each resample, yielding a 95\% percentile confidence interval for the population mean II.

\paragraph{Paired bootstrap.} To test whether the severity gradient differs between importance-based and random eviction, we pair observations by problem (same problem, same sparsity level) and bootstrap the difference in gradients. This controls for problem-level variance in difficulty and sequence length.

\paragraph{Unpaired bootstrap.} We additionally compute per-strategy gradient confidence intervals without pairing, to verify that the importance-based gradient is significantly greater than 1 (i.e., phase modulation exists) independently of the random baseline.

All tests yield $p < 0.0001$ at every sparsity level, confirming that the observed phase--strategy interactions are not artifacts of sampling variability.\footnote{Experiments were conducted on a server with 4$\times$ NVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada 96\,GB GPUs, with one model--sparsity configuration per GPU.}

